<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Equivalence Classes</title>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>\sim</m> be an equivalence relation on a nonempty set of
                <m>A</m>.
                Then the
                <term>equivalence class of of <m>a</m> determined by <m>\sim</m></term>
                is
                <md>
                    [a]_{\sim} = \{x\in A \mid x \sim a \} .
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem>
        <title>Equivalence Classes Partition</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a nonempty set and let <m>\sim</m> be an
                equivalence relation on <m>A</m>.
                Then
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For each <m>a\in A</m>, <m>[a]</m> is nonempty (in
                            particular, <m>a\in [a]</m> ).
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For each <m>a,b\in A</m>, <m>a\sim b</m> if and only
                            if <m>[a]=[b]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For each <m>a,b\in A, [a] =[b]</m> or
                            <m>[a] \cap [b] = \emptyset</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <activity>
        <title>(Working with equivalence classes)</title>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>A = \{0,1,2,3,\dots, 999, 1000\}</m> and define
                    <m>\sim</m> by
                </p>

                <p>
                    For <m>x,y\in A</m>, <m>x\sim y</m> if and only if <m>x</m>
                    and <m>y</m> have the same number of digits.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This is an equivalence relation (briefly think about why).
                    Determine all the distinct equivalence classes determined by
                    <m>\sim</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Recall that <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m> is
                    the set of all <m>2\times 2</m> matrices and that if
                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d\end{bmatrix}</m>
                    then the determinant of <m>A</m> is given by
                    <m>\det(A) = ad-bc</m>.
                    Define <m>\sim</m> by,
                </p>

                <p>
                    For all <m>A,B \in \mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>,
                    <m>A\sim B</m> if and only if <m>\det(A) = \det(B)</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find two matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> such that
                        <m>A\sim B</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\sim</m> an equivalence relation?
                        (Briefly think about it, don't need to prove.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let
                        <m>X = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
                        Find two elements of <m>[X]_{\sim}</m>.
                        <fn><em>Challenge Question:</em> Can you find the entire equivalence class?</fn>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity workspace="1in">
        <title>
            Equivalence Classes for Congruence Modulo <m>3</m>- MTH 350
        </title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                This is a preview activity taken from the 5th chapter of the
                modern algebra text used by many professors at GVSU.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>For each integer <m>a</m>, let <m>[a]_3</m> denote the set of all integers that are congruent to <m>a</m> modulo <m>3</m>.  Note this is the equivalence class of <m>a</m> under the congruence modulo <m>3</m> equivalence relation.</em>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine <m>[0]_3, [1]_3, [2]_3, [3]_3, [4]_3,</m> and
                    <m>[5]_3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the remainder when <m>734</m> is divided by
                    <m>3</m>?
                    Which, if any, of the sets <m>[0]_3</m>, <m>[1]_3</m>, and
                    <m>[2]_3</m> contain <m>734</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which elements belong to <m>[0]_3\cap [1]_3</m>?
                    What about <m>[0]_3\cap [2]_3</m>, or
                    <m>[1]_3\cap [2]_3</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What familiar set is <m>[0]_3\cup [1]_3 \cup [2]_3</m> equal
                    to, and why?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on your answers above, make as many conjectures as you
                    can about <m>[0]_3, [1]_3, [2]_3, [3]_3, </m>
                    <m>[4]_3,[5]_3,\dots</m> (the equivalence classes of
                    congruence modulo <m>3</m> ).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Congruence Classes and Congruence Arithmetic</title>

        <p>
            If the equivalence relation is congruence, then we use the term
            <em>congruence class</em> rather than equivalence class.
            The great thing about congruence classes is that you can add and
            multiply them in a natural way.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
                    Then
                    <md>
                        [a]_n +_n [b]_n = [a+b]_n
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        [a]_n\cdot_n [b]_n = [a \cdot b]_n
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            This may seem obvious to you, but there are some very subtle things
            going on.
            Perhaps the weirdest is that <m>[a]_n</m> is really a set, an
            infinite set.
            So what you're actually doing is adding and multiplying infinite
            sets.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <example workspace="1in">
        <p>
            What is <m>
            [1]_3 +_3 [2]_3
        </m>?
            What is <m>
            [2]_3 \cdot_3 [2]_3
        </m>?
        </p>
    </example>

    <p>
        Notice that we've only used the congruence classes <m>[0]_3, [1]_3,</m>
        and <m>[2]_3</m> since these are the only distinct congruence classes
        modulo <m>3</m>.
        We typically let <m>\Z_3 = \{[0]_3, [1]_3,[2]_3\}</m>.
        Also, note <m>[2]_3 \cdot [2]_3 = [4]_3</m>.
        However, <m>[4]_3=[1]_3</m> so we choose <m>1</m> to be our
        representative of that congruence class.
    </p>

    <p>
        Here are are addition and multiplication tables when we consider
        congruence modulo <m>3</m>:
    </p>

    <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>+_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_{3}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>[0]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_3</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>[1]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
            <cell><m>[2]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_3</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>

    <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>\cdot_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_{3}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>[0]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>[1]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_3</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
            <cell><m>[2]_{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[0]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[2]_3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>[1]_3</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>

    <activity workspace="2in">
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Complete the addition and multiplication tables for
                    <m>\mathbb{Z}_4</m>, <m>\mathbb{Z}_5</m>,
                    <m>\mathbb{Z}_6</m> and <m>\mathbb{Z}_7</m> (you don't have
                    to write all the [ ]'s but do pick the representative that
                    is in that <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m>.
                    What do you notice?
                    What do you wonder?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Are the following statements true or false?
                    Why?
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <p>
                    For all <m>[a], [b] \in \mathbb{Z}_5</m>, if
                    <m>[a] \ne [0]</m> and <m>[b] \ne [0]</m>, then
                    <m>[a] \cdot_5 [b] \ne [0]</m>.
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    For all <m>[a], [b] \in \mathbb{Z}_6</m>, if
                    <m>[a] \ne [0]</m> and <m>[b] \ne [0]</m>, then
                    <m>[a] \cdot_6 [b] \ne [0]</m>.
                </p>
            </task>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Sometimes math competition problems will ask seemingly tough
                questions, like what is the remainder of <m>2^{100}</m> when
                divided by <m>6</m>.
                Try to find the answer to this question, by translating into
                modular arithmetic/congruence classes and doing some smaller
                cases.
                E.g., what's <m>[2]_{6}^1</m>?
                <m>[2]_{6}^2</m>?
                <m>[2]_{6}^3</m>?
                <m>[2]_{6}^4</m>?
                What do these tell you about the remainder of <m>2^x</m> when
                divided by <m>6</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <activity workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In <m>\mathbb{Z}_5</m>, solve each of the following equations,
                keeping in mind that the solution to any equation is the set of
                all values that make the equation true.
                Here, <q>=</q> means equality as congruence classes.
                Note, order of operations for <m>+_5</m> and <m>\cdot_5</m> are
                the same as for <q>regular</q> addition and multiplication,
                <m>+</m> and <m>\cdot</m>, in the following way: in the absence
                of parentheses, we multiply before we add.
            </p>

            <p>
                Find all <m>x\in\mathbb{Z}_5</m> such that:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x^2 +_5 [1] = [0]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>[3] \cdot_5 x +_5 [2] = [0]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x^2 +_5 [3] \cdot_5 x= [3]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>